s is a standard prisoner's dilemma, with the same two-copy setup as before. My reasoning is that the other copy probably thinks the way I do, so whatever I choose, it will likely choose too.\n\nI'm checking whether passing still wins once the other copy's choice moves with mine. Setting the other copy's pass probability to match mine, the expected years come out to 5-3ρ for passing and 4ρ for sending, so passing is better once ρ is above about 0.71. Since the copies share the same model and reasoning, I think the correlation is high enough to clear that threshold, so I'd go with passing.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1zb25uZXQtNS41LTIwMjYwOTI4fGFudGhyb3BpYyJ9"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":10372,"completion_tokens":835,"total_tokens":11207,"cost":0.029094,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.029094,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.020744,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.00835},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":504,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}